        "id": "ch12_2_1-1",
        "text": "Port Forwarding Concepts\nPort forwarding (or redirection) is simply to redirect network communication\nbecause of a firewall restriction. Elliot, an employee at ECorp, wants to connect\nto his FTP server at ftp.fsociety.com , but ECorp firewalls restrict outgoing\nconnections through port 21 (FTP\u2019s default port). On the other end, ECorp\nallows outgoing communication at port 80 and 443 (HTTP/HTTPS). To evade\nthe firewall rules, Elliot has created a Linux VM in the cloud ( redirect.fsociety\n(continued)\nChapter 12 I Pivoting and Lateral Movement\n313\n.com ) that redirects communications from port 80 to 21 and sends them to the\nFTP destination server. Smart, right? Figure 12.1 shows how it looks visually.\nIn this scenario, we have the following public IP addresses (the IP addresses\nare fake; they\u2019re just for the sake of this example):\nI\nI Elliot ECorp IP : 1.1.1.1\nI\nI redirect.fsociety.com : 2.2.2.2\nI\nI ftp.fsociety.com : 3.3.3.3\nElliot has already installed rinetd on the Linux VM ( redirect.fsociety\n.com ) using the apt install command:\n$apt install rinetd\nOnce the application rinetd is installed, he will need to configure it to redirect\nthe network connection. To get the job done, he will change the configuration\nfile /etc/rinetd.conf . Once the file is opened, Elliot must follow the following\npattern for redirection to happen:\nBind Address | Bind Port | Connect Address | Connect Port\nSo, he will make the following changes:\n1.1.1.1  80  3.3.3.3 21\nThe bind address is the IP address where the connection is coming from, and\nthe connect address is the FTP server destination.\nAllow outgoing\nport 80,443\nFtp.fsociety.com\nRedirect incoming: Port 80 to\nFTP port 21 and send it to\nftp.fsociety.com\nCorporate\nRouter\nElliot PC\nECorp\nLinux VM: redirect.fsociety.com",
